## Solution

Let $s = \sin t$, so $\cos^2 t = 1 - s^2$.

**Step 1: Simplify the bracket.**

$$\sin^4 t - (1+\sin t)(\cos^2 t - 2) = s^4 - (1+s)((1-s^2)-2) = s^4 - (1+s)(-1-s^2)$$
$$= s^4 + (1+s)(1+s^2) = s^4 + 1 + s^2 + s + s^3 = s^4 + s^3 + s^2 + s + 1.$$

Denote $Q(s) = s^4+s^3+s^2+s+1 = \dfrac{s^5-1}{s-1}$.

**Step 2: Simplify the LHS.** Since $\cos^4 t = (1-s^2)^2 = 1-2s^2+s^4$,

$$-5s^5\cos^4 t = -5s^5(1-2s^2+s^4) = -5s^5+10s^7-5s^9.$$

So the LHS becomes
$$F(s) = 7s^{31}+8s^{13}+(-5s^5+10s^7-5s^9)-10s^7+5s^5-2 = 7s^{31}+8s^{13}-5s^9-2.$$

**Step 3: Polynomial division mod $Q$.** The identity requires
$$F(s) = P(s)\,Q(s) + R(s), \qquad \deg R < 4.$$

Since $Q$ is monic of degree 4, the remainder $R$ of $F$ upon division by $Q$ is **unique** (independent of $P$). So there is exactly one such $R$.

Since $Q(s) = (s^5-1)/(s-1)$, at every root $\zeta$ of $Q$ (a primitive 5th root of unity) we have $\zeta^5 = 1$, hence $s^5 \equiv 1 \pmod{Q}$. Therefore:

$$s^{31} = s^{5\cdot 6+1} \equiv s, \qquad s^{13} = s^{5\cdot 2+3} \equiv s^3, \qquad s^9 = s^{5\cdot 1+4} \equiv s^4.$$

Thus
$$F(s) \equiv 7s + 8s^3 - 5s^4 - 2 \pmod{Q}.$$

**Step 4: Reduce $s^4$.** From $Q(s)=0$: $s^4 = -s^3-s^2-s-1$, so
$$-5s^4 \equiv 5s^3+5s^2+5s+5.$$

Therefore
$$R(s) \equiv 7s + 8s^3 + 5s^3 + 5s^2 + 5s + 5 - 2 = 13s^3 + 5s^2 + 12s + 3.$$

**Verification.** $\deg R = 3 < 4$. ✓ The remainder is unique, so this is the only $R$.

$$\boxed{R(x) = 13x^3 + 5x^2 + 12x + 3}$$

